<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Nixon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Richard M. Nixon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard Nixon was the 37th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, from 1969 to 1974. He withdrew American forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, pursued détente with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and, with Henry Kissinger, opened relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in February 1972. Facing impeachment over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watergate-scandal">Watergate scandal</a>, he became the only US president to resign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-nixon.jpg" alt="Richard Nixon" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Department of Defense. Department of the Army. Office of the Deputy Chief of Staff for Operations. U.S. Army Audiovisual Center. (ca. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Nixon_presidential_portrait_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Richard Nixon</b>’s visit in February 1972 began a realignment that led to full diplomatic relations with the United States in 1979.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 20th century the presidency became the centre of the federal government, and in 1974 <b>Richard Nixon</b> resigned rather than face impeachment over the Watergate scandal.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>In a televised address, <b>Richard Nixon</b> said he would resign the presidency at noon the next day.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Citing progress in the Paris peace talks, <b>Richard Nixon</b> halted US bombing and mining of North Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard Nixon</b> won the presidency that November.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-31">31 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Birthplace of Richard Nixon, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Weill Cornell Medical Center, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.89,-117.82&amp;year=1965" aria-label="Birthplace of Richard Nixon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1546 935 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Richard Nixon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M1996 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M1996 1205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.89,-117.82&amp;year=1965">What was here in 1965? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
